{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And he expired and was gathered</b> — and death is not stated regarding him. And our Rabbis said (Ta'anit 5a), 'Jacob our father did not die' — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 49:33). Now according to the opinion of our Rabbis, Jacob himself mentioned death (Genesis 48:21): 'Behold, I am dying, and may Elohim be with you' — perhaps he himself did not know, or he did not wish to bring honor to his name. Likewise (Genesis 50:15), 'And Joseph's brothers saw that their father was dead' — for them he was dead, or perhaps they did not know this at all. And the meaning of this midrash is that the souls of the righteous are bound in the bond of life, and it hovers over him all day, donning its second garment, so that he does not walk about naked — as with Jacob — or it clothes itself at appointed times. And this matter will be understood in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 152b) and in tractate Ketubot (103a)." "And the book of Genesis is completed, with the account of the words of the fathers. To declare first things and new things before they spring forth in hearts. And to Him who forms every first thing, riding upon the steppes. Abundant praises and thanksgivings to the myriads. And for Him were ordained the deeds and the causes. He reveals depths and exalted understandings. And brings thoughts out to light. He who leads me in the way of righteousness amid pathways. He who deals kindly with the guilty." "Finished and completed, praise to the Creator of the world."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:39.625854556Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}